Z||000000||zebrex||03-24-2000||06:58 PM||||Will 205/50/15 fit on the stock rims of my 88 formula? there no taller than my 205/60/15s just about 1.5 wider anybody have them on there car?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-24-2000||07:12 PM||||Not enough info. <P>you need to take the tire information for that specific tire. go to the makers web site or hard catalog, and see what rim width the tire is intended for. An inch and a half wider is likely to much for those tires on your rims.<P>Keep in mind:<P>tire width number is NOT tread width. It's the section width at the center of the sidewall when mounted on a specific size rim. (Tread width is NOT printed on the tire. It is only available from catalog data or direct measurement.)<P>Tires generally can be safely mounted on a rim +- 1/2 inch of the test rim width. The limits vary from one tire to the next. You have to check the width limits for each individual tire design. Some very ridgid tires are only certified on the test rim width. Others can handle +- 3/4 inch.<P>If the rim is not in spec for the tire it will cause problems. Some may be subtle but there will be problems. wear problems, handling problems, even sudden blowouts are all real issues.<P>No responsible tireman will mount tires on off spec rims. They don't want to get sued when there are problems. Anyone who does mount the tires on off spec rims voids any and all warranties on the tires as well.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||zebrex||03-24-2000||07:27 PM||||section width is 7.5 on 205/50/15 I know my car has two different width rims but I dont know the width. What is the stock tire size on 88 formula. besides tire man isnt worried about rim size issues hes worried about rubbing the front fenders. and the 50 series represents highdth to width asspect ratio (sorry about spelling )||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||03-24-2000||08:45 PM||||I dont understand how they could be taller. 205 is the width--60 is the height ratio of the width. therefore 50 should be lower. This is what i have been told. Also i see no reason why they would not fit. Your car should sit lower with the 50.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-24-2000||08:49 PM||||I have a Formula too. Original wheel sizes are:<BR>front-15x6<BR>rear-15x7<BR>Stock tire sizes are:<BR>front-205/60/15<BR>rear-215/60/15<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-24-2000||09:20 PM||||If your front end is stock (not lowered), then I'm 99% sure those tires will work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Klown||03-24-2000||10:41 PM||||I'm using 225/60-15's on all four corners of my lowered 86GT.  The fronts barely touch the  inner fender wells during full turns and the rears have no problem.  These tires are both wider and taller than the tires you plan to put on your Fiero so I don't why you were told they wouldn't fit unless it's because that tire width is to narrow or wide for the rim width.||||reg||1
